<!--
  Reference only -- this is infrastructure config, not SQL DDL.
  Deploy to config.d/ on every node in FRA, MUC, and HAM (e.g. via ConfigMap +
  volume mount on the official ClickHouse operator's ClickHouseCluster
  podTemplate/containerTemplate). In the kind demo this same shape is injected
  as spec.settings.extraConfig by scripts/patch-federation.sh (plaintext) and
  scripts/setup-tls.sh (TLS); see those for the live values.

  ── Region VIP per shard (not per-pod hosts) ──────────────────────────────────
  Each shard is one region, addressed by a SINGLE endpoint that fronts every
  ClickHouse replica in that region -- a Kubernetes Service (ClusterIP /
  LoadBalancer) or a load-balancer hostname -- NOT an enumerated list of pod
  hostnames. Any node in a region has otel_regional (created ON CLUSTER), so a
  Distributed shard only needs to reach *some* node there; the VIP load-balances
  and, in a real deployment, the operator keeps its endpoint set in sync as
  replicas scale. Result: adding/removing REPLICAS within a region needs NO
  change here. Only adding/removing a whole REGION (a shard) does -- and that
  also means updating the default.regionToShard dictionary.
  Constraint: a VIP must front exactly ONE region's nodes. Shard N must always
  be region N or the dictGet(regionToShard, region) sharding key mispruning.

  ── Keep the home-region shard on localhost ───────────────────────────────────
  This file is therefore NOT byte-identical across nodes: on a node in region X,
  point region X's shard at <host>localhost</host><port>9440</port> so ClickHouse
  marks it is_local=1 and reads it via ReadFromLocal (no network hop, no TLS).
  Routing the home region through its own VIP would work but forfeit that
  optimization. The example below is the FRA node's copy (FRA -> localhost);
  the MUC and HAM copies are analogous (their own region -> localhost).

  ── Cluster secret (per-user RBAC across DCs) ─────────────────────────────────
  <secret> turns on ClickHouse's secure inter-server protocol for this cluster.
  With it, a cross-DC query propagates the ORIGINAL user and their grants to the
  remote shard, and the remote shard enforces that user's RBAC. WITHOUT it, the
  remote subquery runs as the `default` user and the initiator's identity is
  never sent -- trust then rests entirely on locking down the inter-server port.
  The secret MUST be byte-identical on every node. Store it in a K8s Secret and
  inject via env (<secret from_env="CH_CLUSTER_SECRET"/>) rather than committing
  a literal; the demo scripts generate one at runtime (see setup.sh).
  Two caveats the secret imposes (see README "Cross-region RBAC"):
    - The propagated user is matched BY NAME, so cross-DC users must exist
      identically on every node (schemas/04_rbac runs per DC); a missing user
      on a remote DC resets the connection (NETWORK_ERROR: Broken pipe).
    - ClickHouse propagates the user identity but does NOT enable its ROLES on
      remote shards -- only privileges granted DIRECTLY to the user are enforced
      remotely. Grant cross-DC readers directly on the user, not via a role.

  Shard order matters: shard_num is 1-based, so
    shard 1 = FRA, shard 2 = MUC, shard 3 = HAM.
  Each shard carries a <name> (FRA/MUC/HAM). This surfaces as
  system.clusters.shard_name, which the default.regionToShard dictionary reads
  to build the region -> (shard_num - 1) sharding-key map used by
  otel_global. The <name> values MUST match the region column values.
-->
<clickhouse>
  <remote_servers>
    <global>
      <!-- Propagate the querying user + grants to remote shards; inject the
           same value on every node (from_env from a K8s Secret in production). -->
      <secret from_env="CH_CLUSTER_SECRET"/>
      <shard>
        <name>FRA</name>
        <!-- Home region on this (FRA) node: localhost => is_local, no TLS hop. -->
        <replica><host>localhost</host><port>9440</port><secure>1</secure></replica>
      </shard>
      <shard>
        <name>MUC</name>
        <!-- Region VIP: one Service/LB fronting all MUC replicas. -->
        <replica><host>muc-clickhouse.muc.svc.cluster.local</host><port>9440</port><secure>1</secure></replica>
      </shard>
      <shard>
        <name>HAM</name>
        <replica><host>ham-clickhouse.ham.svc.cluster.local</host><port>9440</port><secure>1</secure></replica>
      </shard>
    </global>
  </remote_servers>
</clickhouse>
